content.text:
Only the platform rider drift remains — diff 16.5px over 30 frames. This is a real gameplay defect: moving platform riding is loose. Earlier my manual analysis showed the problem: player falls 25px/frame (from standstill vy after 1 frame of gravity = 25) — while riding the platform, the ride-delta is applied first (`pl.x += rider.dx`), then player.update pulls gravity: vy = 25 → collideY: player bottom 25px below plat top... plat h=14: embedded 11px: resolvePlatforms pushes out min-axis: penT = 11 vs penB = (plat.y+14) - playerTop = 14+11 = 25 vs penL = right edge - plat.x... resolution horizontal overlap: player 20 wide vs plat 96: penL ~ (pR - plat.x) which is 20+10 = 30ish; penT 11 min → push up ✓ → land y plat-top ✓ rider re-established. So per frame, net position = ride dx + dy... vertical only; horizontal diff should stay...

Compute: plat speed 105 px/s = 1.75px/frame — plus rider drift each frame if between "rider set" (end of frame) and "rider apply" (start of next frame): plat moves FIRST (dx 1.75), then rider applied: plat.x += 1.75; player.x += 1.75: aligned. Then in player.update: land again, rider re-set to same plat... Hmm aligned.

But wait — the panel: dy: plat.y stays constant (axis x) → fine.

So why 16.5 drift over 30 frames = 0.55px/frame = 1/3 of ride speed? Because the rider flag DROPS every few frames (grounded false toggling): the vertical bouncing of grounded due to plat.h=14 and vy dominance: player embedded 11px each frame: resolve pushes to plat.y - 0.01... rider found (|bottom - plat.y|<4 ✓)... suppose one frame the collision landed the player on the TILE GROUND row 15 below?? plat row 13 (y=416): bottom up to 430+... ground at 480 — no.

16.5px over 30 frames = exactly HALF speed (1.75 * 30 = 52.5; player got 52.5 - 16.5 = 36?? hmm diff 16.5). Actually note the test measured `diff = |(p.x + 10) - plat.x|`: p.x measured AFTER 30 frames: diff 16.5 — precise values depend on substeps.

Deeper: during `player.update`, before Y move there's X move (vx 0) fine. vy accumulating: each frame rider apply dy=0: y = plat.y - h - 0.01 stable-ish.

But WAIT — issue: the player's Y-collide runs BEFORE resolvePlatforms; land() sets rider only if |footY - plat.y| < 4: footY = y+30 where after collide延续了... if embedded 11px below top, bottom = plat.y+11: |11| > 4 → RIDER NULL!! (land() only sets rider when aligned). Then ground-recheck branch also calls ground() which requires |footY - plat.y| < 4 → NULL. THEN resolvePlatforms pushes to aligned... but rider already null → next frame no ride dx applied → drift!

Sequence each frame: fall 25 → embedded 11 → rider null; resolve pushes up to aligned top; rider stays null. Next frame: rider null: no ride dx; player falls again 25; embedded; pushed up BUT plat moved 1.75 left: player lost 1.75px/frame → 30 frames = 52.5px?? measured 16.5 — some frames rider applied (aligned after resolve, next frame's ground recheck at aligned y: supY = boot+1: solid ✓ ground() rider set ✓). So alternating 1/n of the time — drift ~16.5 over 30 → rides ~2/3 of frames.

Root fix: ground()/land()'s rider threshold `< 4` too strict given the per-frame fall of |vy|*dt. Instead the threshold shouldn't matter: rider should be chosen during the collision itself. But easier robust fix: in ground(), extend window to max(4, |vy*dt| + 4)? vy at time of ground call could be large — non-ideal.

Better: set rider based on resolvePlatforms push direction: when resolve pushes player UP against platform top (gdir 1) → set rider directly. Also in collideY's platform crossing detection (swipe) — implement properly:

Add to Player.collideY a platform sweep: for each platform, if horizontal overlap and oldFoot <= plat.y+2 && newFoot >= plat.y (falling) → snap & land. This closes the tunneling AND properly sets rider since land() runs aligned.

Implement:
```
// platforms: swept landing
var oldY = this.y - (Y move already applied?) 
```
collideY called AFTER y move... we don't retain old. Restructure: in update's substep loop, record prevY before `this.y += vy*sdt`, pass to collideY(w, prevY). Then:

```
Player.prototype.collideY = function (w, prevY) {
  ...
  // moving platforms: swept landing on top face
  for (var i = 0; i < w.plats.length; i++) {
    var p = w.plats[i];
    if (r.x + r.w <= p.x || r.x >= p.x + p.w) continue;
    if (this.gdir > 0) {
      var top = p.y;
      var of = prevY + r.h, nf = r.y + r.h;
      if (of <= top + 0.5 && nf >= top) { this.y = top - r.h - 0.01; this.land(w); break; }
      // pushed from below (rising) — handled by resolve
    } else {
      var bot = p.y + p.h;
      var oh = prevY, nh = r.y;
      if (oh >= bot - 0.5 && nh <= bot) { this.y = bot + 0.01; this.land(w); break; }
    }
  }
```
Then land() sets rider when aligned ✓ (it will be).

Still the frame sequence: player rides: rider set; NEXT frame: plat moves 1.75: rider.dx applied: aligned ✓; player.update: vy = gravity*1 = 25: falls: swept check: oldFoot aligned = plat.y - 0.01 <= top+0.5 ✓; newFoot = plat.y+24 >= top ✓ → snap & land ✓ rider ✓ — every frame stable & perfect tracking.

But careful with the sweep check for the TILE floor (row 15) — tiles handled by solidAt; platform sweep only for plats ✓.

Also guard: platform SPECIFIC check only if vy direction along gdir (falling onto). If player jumps up through platform from below: no crossing of top face ✓ passes through ✓ (since from below: oldFoot < top... oldFoot happens below → of > top → no landing ✓ passes through: but resolvePlatforms will push when overlapping (rising past plat: bottom > top while overlapping → pushes UP? min-axis: penT = nf - top large-ish, penB = platBottom - playerTop: if player mostly below center... could snap atop while jumping up through — classic bonk-on-through platform. Platforms are SOLID plates (both directions) — jumping up through it from below would resolve pushing up — acceptable (solid block behavior; levels designed so).

BUT resolvePlatforms could wrongly snap player on TOP when jumping beside/going UP under a plat he just crossed horizontally... standard solid-plate behavior; ok with slow plats.

Also X sweep similar minor (plat pushes player sideways): handle in resolvePlatforms min-axis ✓ with its existing logic; X penetration motion 1.75px/frame ok.

Additionally, to be safer, set fixed `steps` substep min... ok.

Implement:
- update(): pass prevY through substeps: store `var prevY = this.y;` before each `this.y += this.vy * sdt;` then `this.collideY(w, prevY)`.
- collideY signature (w, prevY).
- Also the "moving down/non-moving" branches unchanged; add plat sweep AFTER tile branches; if tile-land happened, still run sweep? order: do tiles first (if landed skip sweep? tiles floor level plat none). Simplify: run plat sweep only if !landedOnTile.

Edit entities.js accordingly.

